many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism a common  challenge to stoicism particularly on  their views about nature and living in  accordance with nature stems from using  a passage from Friedrich Nietzsche's  Beyond Good and Evil and saying didn't  Nietzsche prove that the Stoics are  wrong the short answer to this question  is no Nietzsche did not prove that the  Stoics have nature or living in  accordance with nature wrong but there's  a lot more to say about the topic I  think that in a case like this it's very  helpful to look at what Nietzsche  actually says in this this passage which  is early on in beyond good and evil it's  section 9 of chapter 1 on the prejudices  of philosophers so here's what he  actually says you want to live according  to nature oh you noble Stoics what  fraudulent words think of a being such  as nature as prodigal beyond measure  indifferent beyond measure without aims  or intentions without mercy or justice  at once fruitful and bear it and  uncertain think of indifference itself  as a power how could you live according  to such indifference so that's one  important passage he continues to live  is this not precisely wanting to be  other than this nature is living not  valuating preferring being unjust being  limited wanting to be different and even  if your imperative live according to  nature men at bottom the same thing as  live according to life how could you not  do that why make a principle of what you  yourselves are and must be so that's  another important part of this passage  then he continues and says the truth of  it is however quite different while you  rapturously pose as deriving the Canon  of your law from nature you want  something quite the reverse of that you  strange actors and self deceivers your  pride wants to  prescribed your morality your ideal to  nature yes to nature itself and  incorporate them in it you demand that  nature should be nature according to the  stoah and would like to make all  existence exist only after your own  image as a tremendous eternal  glorification and universalization of  stoicism so these are some of the  criticisms that nietzsche is making in  that passage that people take as a  challenge to stoic doctrine now before  we consider each of these three points  in turn let's think for a moment about a  big assumption that people are making in  raising this very question does having a  criticism that some famous philosopher  gives of a particular doctrine  automatically mean that that doctrine is  thereby refuted nullified disproved the  answer is no because the person who's  making the criticism could actually be  off base or could be talking about  something quite different which happens  to be the case with this particular  passage so think about the first thing  that Nietzsche said in here he has a  very different conception of nature than  what the Stoics doing totally  understandable Nietzsche is coming from  very different philosophical basis he is  elaborating a very different  philosophical position so this is sort  of like somebody you know in one genre  of music saying wow you don't play it  all the way that I do his notion of  nature he says is a being that is  prodigal without measure and different  beyond measure without aims and  intentions without mercy or justice and  that just isn't what the Stoics are  calling nature so just saying I mean  this and you mean that therefore you're  wrong that's not a very good refutation  the second part of the passage makes two  interesting moves one is to say that  here's what living actually is and so  that has to be the opposite of nature of  course notice nature as I need to have  understood it and articulated it to you  so as so it could just say well I don't  agree with what you think nature is then  he says what you Stoics are saying is  really just vacuous I mean if you if you  mean you know living in accordance with  life how can you not do that everybody's  doing that already so you're really just  uttering a truism or a tautology that  doesn't mean anything  and again you have to ask yourself is is  that what the Stoics actually mean or is  nietzsche caricaturing them his third  criticism I think really gets to the  heart of the matter he is saying listen  you Stoics I know that that you have a  different conception of nature than I do  I whom I'm actually right about it and  what you're doing is kind of  intellectual sleight of hand you're  reading into nature what it is that  you're wanting to find in nature and  then surprise surprise you actually do  find it so what you're doing is in  essence just assuming it's like a  circular form of reasoning or if you  like of inquiry and again you have to  ask yourself is nietzsche actually you  know depicting the Stoics in a fair  light here I think just a brief reading  of stoic text shows that Nietzsche is  actually off base on this one there may  be criticisms that one can give of  stoicism and of certain assumptions that  they're making but this isn't it  as a last note I'd like to say we can  generalize this a little bit further  it's not just about Nietzsche and the  Stoics and this particular passage  anytime that you're bringing up somebody  who is criticizing somebody else you  want to make sure that the criticism is  in fact on base they're not attributing  to those other people something that  they're not saying they are criticizing  them for things that you know you don't  have to automatically buy into their  system in order to say that makes sense  and that isn't the case here and that  isn't the case with many of the  criticisms not only of stoicism but  other philosophical positions you want  to be very careful in adopting other  philosophers critical assessments of  people whose philosophical basis systems  and orientations they do not share 